Runs for the follow-ups to the liveness work (branch live): zero read size, deadline and connect timeout refused; an empty read counted as a tick.  On the build host in localhost/b8perf-lean with Lean v4.34.1.

Everything, from a remote dir with its .lake removed first, at the commit in final/COMMIT:
$ ssh buildhost 'rm --recursive --force lean-http-live/.lake lean-http-live/out && mkdir --parents lean-http-live/out'
$ scp run-all.sh ../proofs/core-run.sh replays.sh replays/*.lean buildhost:lean-http-live/out/
$ LEAN_HTTP_REMOTE_DIR=lean-http-live scripts/remote.sh bash out/run-all.sh final
  final/build.txt     lake build from scratch, no warnings
  final/axioms.txt    #print axioms for 37 theorems (the 36 before and step_empty_received), each
                      [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]
  final/unit.txt, codec.txt, traces.txt, props-seed{1,2}.txt, explore-10.txt, replays.txt

Compared with ../live/final/: codec and explore-10 identical; unit has six new checks (54, all
passing); traces has the two empty-read traces (44, all passing); the random runs differ, because
the generator now also reports empty reads while a read is outstanding.  With that one generator
option taken out again (no-empty-reads/, no-empty-reads.diff, run.sh), both random runs are
identical to ../live/final/ but for the echoed command line: on the events the generator produced
before, the core behaves as it did.  The replays are those of ../live/ and give the same results.

Positive control (mutant/): with the core's empty-read change undone (mutant.diff), both new traces
fail, the random runs fail "one read in flight" at run 10 of seed 1, and with that property skipped
they fail the new "an empty read is a tick" at the same run.  (The "exit status" lines there are of
the grep and head at the end of each pipeline, not of core-tests.)

Zero settings over loopback (zero-probe/), net-client against python -m http.server:
  before.txt  probe.sh on the tree before the change: connectTimeoutMs=0 connected in all ten calls
              (loopback completes the connect before a zero-millisecond timer); connectStaggerMs=0
              worked; deadlineMs=0 failed at once with a deadline timeout, not sent, and with a host
              name gave rounds=0, i.e. the name lookup was started and abandoned.
  after.txt   probe-after.sh, with CTR_EXTRA='--add-host two:127.0.0.1 --add-host two:127.0.0.2':
              connectTimeoutMs=0 and deadlineMs=0 now fail as invalid requests before any I/O; a
              zero stagger over a name with two addresses (the first refusing) connects to the
              second like the default stagger does.  That first address refuses at once, so this
              does not show the stagger itself; a silent first address needs packet filtering the
              container does not offer.
